"MuCo: Multi-turn Contrastive Learning for Multimodal Embedding Model (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.06393v1)\nciteturn27148view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.06393v1\",\"lineno\":721}); Total lines: 1892\nL598@P5: query-target pairs are randomly shuffled per batch instance\nL599@P5: to prevent positional bias. In our adaptive prompting strat\u0002egy (§4.2), we split the text sentence into words based on\nL600@P5: spaces and then randomly mask 50% of the words using\nL601@P5: a uniform sampling strategy. If not specified, we employ\nL602@P5: Qwen2-VL-7B for image captioning in §4.2\nL603@P5: Datasets. In Tab. 1, we use the proposed M3T dataset of\nL605@P5: M3T (20%) with 1M and M3T (60%) with 3M samples.\nL606@P5: We also utilize mmE5 [8] dataset with 560K and the Mega\u0002Pairs [69] dataset with 26M samples for comparison. The\nL607@P5: details of these datasets are described in the Sec. B. For the\nL608@P5: fine-tuning stage, models are trained on two benchmarks:\nL609@P5: MMEB [30] and M-BEIR [62]. MMEB is a benchmark\nL610@P5: containing 36 individual datasets across four categories:\nL611@P5: classification, visual question answering (VQA), retrieval,\nL612@P5: and visual grounding. MMEB reframes all four categories\nL613@P5: into ranking problems with a maximum of 1,000 candi\u0002dates. The benchmark provides training samples from 20\nL614@P5: of the 36 datasets (considered In-Distribution), while the re\u0002maining 16 are reserved exclusively for evaluation (Out-of\u0002Distribution). M-BEIR is a benchmark focusing specifically\nL615@P5: on multimodal retrieval performance. It consists of 16 re\u0002trieval datasets across 8 retrieval tasks. In our experiments,\nL616@P5: we fine-tune our models on the respective training set for\nL617@P5: each benchmark and evaluate them on the corresponding\nL618@P5: test set.\nL619@P5: 5.2. Main Results\nL620@P5: We provide the evaluation results on MMEB in Tab. 1 and\nL621@P5: M-BEIR in Tab. 2. MuCo achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA)\nL622@P5: performance on the MMEB and M-BEIR benchmark across\nL623@P5-6: different model scales. This superior performance stemsTable 3. Pretraining dataset variants. ‘None’ represents fine\u0002tuning without pretraining. We compare against other pretraining\nL624@P6: datasets (mmE5 and MegaPair) and our M3T at various scales.\nL625@P6: The ’mmE5’ entry under the ’Multi-turn’ section is generated us\u0002ing images from the single-turn mmE5 synth dataset.\nL626@P6: Pre-training Dataset Samples ZS\nL627@P6: MMEB\nL628@P6: FT\nL629@P6: MMEB\nL630@P6: Single-turn\nL631@P6: None – 68.5\nL632@P6: mmE5 [8] 0.6M 55.6 68.6\nL633@P6: MegaPairs [69] 26M 41.5 68.7\nL634@P6: Multi-turn\nL635@P6: MuCo-§4.1\nL636@P6: mmE5 [8] 0.6M 57.0 69.0\nL637@P6: M3T (20%) 1M 57.1 69.0\nL638@P6: M3T (60%) 3M 57.7 69.2\nL639@P6: M3T 5M 58.2 69.5\nL640@P6: from our multi-turn dialogue structure, which allows the\nL641@P6: model to learn rich context and compounded supervisory\nL642@P6: signals, thereby effectively improving the embeddings. In\nL643@P6: the zero-shot setting, our 7B model (61.6) surpasses the\nL644@P6: previous SOTA mmE5-11B [8] (58.6). In the fine-tuning\nL645@P6: setting, MuCo establishes new SOTA scores in both sub\u00027B and 7B-and-above categories. Specifically, MuCo-2B\nL646@P6: achieves 69.5, outperforming the previous best B3-2B [58]\nL647@P6: (68.1). Similarly, MuCo-7B establishes a new top score of\nL648@P6: 73.6, surpassing B3-7B [58] (72.0) and achieving the high\u0002est OOD score, which indicates superior generalization to\nL649@P6: unseen tasks. This strong performance trend continues on\nL650@P6: the M-BEIR benchmark. MuCo-2B (54.2) and MuCo-7B\nL651@P6: (58.7) ourperform all competing methods. We note their\nL652@P6: robust performance in the complex multi-modal to multi\u0002modal (M → M) setting, achieving top scores of 68.7 (2B)\nL653@P6: and 74.3 (7B).\nL654@P6: 5.3. Empirical Analysis\nL655@P6: This section analyzes our design choices. Unless speci\u0002fied, all experiments use MuCo-2B on MMEB. In tables, ZS\nL656@P6: MMEB denotes zero-shot performance (Precision@1) after\nL657@P6: MuCo pretraining (§4.1), and FT MMEB denotes the perfor\u0002mance (Precision@1) after MuCo fine-tuning (§4.2) on the\nL658@P6: MMEB training set.\nL659@P6: Impact of pretraining dataset. We analyze the impact\nL660@P6: of different pretraining datasets in Tab. 3 by varying pre\u0002training method and dataset. Note that we fix MuCo fine\u0002tuning (§4.2) to all cases for FT MMEB. The results re\u0002veal two key insights. First, MuCo pretraining is highly ef\u0002fective. Training on mmE5 after converting them to our\nL661@P6: multi-turn format outperforms the original single-turn pre\u0002training (69.0 vs. 68.6 FT MMEB), demonstrating the sig\u0002nificant advantage of our richer, multi-turn signal. Sec\u0002ond, M3T is a high-quality dataset that enables large-scale\nL662@P6: MuCo pretraining with clear gains. MuCopretraining perfor\u0002mance improves monotonically from 0.6M to 5M. It implies\nL663@P6: that M3T offers mmE5-level quality at roughly ×10 scale,\nL664@P6: while MegaPairs [69] degrades performance with massive\nL665@P6: data. It is noteworthy that the model without any pretraining\nL666@P6: Table 4. Ablation study for data composition. ‘All’ means the\nL667@P6: full dataset. Subsequent rows show performance after sequentially\nL668@P6: removing task categories: Classification (− CLS), followed by\nL669@P6: global VQA (− global VQA; holistic understanding), local VQA\nL670@P6: (− local VQA; localized details), and creative VQA (− creative\nL671@P6: VQA; creative reasoning). All results are in the zero-shot setting\nL672@P6: on MMEB. GRD stands for visual grounding. The final row is the\nL673@P6: result of training only on the remaining Retrieval (RET) data.\nL674@P6: Setup CLS VQA RET GRD Overall\nL675@P6: All 53.6 59.9 55.2 74.6 58.2\nL676@P6: − CLS 51.7 59.2 54.6 72.5 56.9\nL677@P6: − global VQA 51.1 57.6 54.6 71.5 56.3\nL678@P6: − local VQA 50.8 56.1 54.1 70.2 55.5\nL679@P6: − creative VQA 50.2 55.7 53.7 69.8 55.1\nL680@P6: Table 5. Analysis of scaling batch size vs. turns. We compare\nL681@P6: the baseline method (#turns=1) with increasing batch sizes (1024\nL682@P6: to 8192) against our proposed MuCo method (#turns ≥ 2) with an\nL683@P6: increasing number of turns at a fixed batch size 1024 in a zero-shot\nL684@P6: setting on MMEB. For the 2-turn and 4-turn settings, pairs were\nL685@P6: randomly sampled from the 7 available pairs. The ‘effective batch’\nL686@P6: refers to the total number of query-target pairs (#batch × #turns),\nL687@P6: which amplifies the learning signal by providing more positive and\nL688@P6: negative pairs for the contrastive loss.\nL689@P6: #turns #batch #effective batch PFLOPs MMEB\nL690@P6: 1 1024 1024 17.5 57.1\nL691@P6: 1 2048 2048 35.1 57.3\nL692@P6: 1 4096 4096 70.2 57.4\nL693@P6: 1 7168 7168 122.7 57.5\nL694@P6: 1 8192 8192 140.4 57.8\nL695@P6: 2 1024 2048 17.6 57.4\nL696@P6: 4 1024 4096 17.7 57.7\nL697@P6: 7 1024 7168 18.0 58.2\nL698@P6: (None) achieves 68.5, which is competitive with the mmE5-\nL699@P6: pretrained model (68.6). This shows that MuCo fine-tuning\nL700@P6: alone is strong enough to surpass the previous SOTA B3-2B\nL701@P6: (68.1, Table 1) even without pre-training.\nL702@P6: We analyze each pretraining task’s contribution in Tab. 4.\nL703@P6: Sequentially excluding tasks (− CLS, global/local/creative\nL704@P6: VQA) causes a performance drop in the corresponding\nL705@P6: task. Notably, excluding local VQA also degrades Vi\u0002sual Grounding (GRD), and removing creative VQA further\nL706@P6: lowers overall performance. This confirms each component\nL707@P6: of our synthesized data is beneficial.\nL708@P6: Scaling batch-size vs. turns. We compare scaling the\nL709@P6: batch size and the number of turns in Tab. 5. For the con\u0002ventional method (#turns=1), increasing the batch size from\nL710@P6: 1024 to 8192 yields performance gains (57.1 to 57.8) at\nL711@P6: a prohibitively high computational cost. In contrast, our\nL712@P6: method scales the number of turns (#turns 2 to 7) at a fixed\nL713@P6: 1024 batch size. Notably, our 7-turn model (7168 effective\nL714@P6: batch size) achieves a higher score than the 1-turn base\u0002line with a 7168 batch size (58.2 vs. 57.5) and even sur\u0002passes the larger 8192-batch baseline (57.8) while the 7168-\nL715@P6-7: batch baseline incurs a massive computation overhead. ThisTable 6. Impact of compounded supervision. Preventing causal\nL716@P7: attention to attend earlier contexts leads to a performance drop,\nL717@P7: supporting the importance of compounded supervision.\nL718@P7: Setup ZS MMEB FT MMEB\nL719@P7: w/o compounded supervision 57.3 68.4\nL720@P7: w/ compounded supervision 58.2 69.5\nL721@P7: Table 7. Impact of logit masking. While beneficial in pre-training\nL722@P7: due to its diverse multi-turn data, this strategy is critical for fine\u0002tuning, as it prevents a severe performance collapse caused by in\u0002correctly treating self-augmented pairs as negatives\nL723@P7: Logit masking\nL724@P7: Pretraining Fine-tuning ZS MMEB FT MMEB\nL725@P7: - - 57.7 31.1\nL726@P7: - ✓ 57.7 69.2\nL727@P7: ✓ - 58.2 30.9\nL728@P7: ✓ ✓ 58.2 69.5\nL729@P7: demonstrates that our method provides a far more efficient\nL730@P7: path to achieving the benefits of a larger effective batch.\nL731@P7: Impact of compounded supervision. We conduct an abla\u0002tion study to validate the effectiveness of our compounded\nL732@P7: supervision (i.e., subsequent turns provide a compounded\nL733@P7: supervisory signal that retroactively refines earlier embed\u0002dings), as shown in Tab. 6. We test a variant where this\nL734@P7: accumulation is disabled by modifying the causal attention\nL735@P7: mask in both pretraining and fine-tuning; this forces each\nL736@P7: turn to only attend to the initial image and its own tokens,\nL737@P7: isolating it from the context of preceding turns. The results\nL738@P7: demonstrate our hypothesis: disabling this compounded su\u0002pervision leads to a performance drop (68.4) compared to\nL739@P7: MuCo (69.5). This confirms that our strategy of accumulat\u0002ing supervisory signals across turns is a superior approach\nL740@P7: for learning robust representations.\nL741@P7: Impact of logit masking. We validate the importance of\nL742@P7: our logit masking strategy in Tab. 7, which is critical for\nL743@P7: fine-tuning. Disabling it (e.g. row (1,3) vs. row (2,3) in\nL744@P7: FT MMEB) causes a performance collapse. This is be\u0002cause, during fine-tuning, we construct subsequent turns\nL745@P7: using each counterpart. Without logit masking, the model\nL746@P7: treats these semantically overlapped pairs as negative pairs,\nL747@P7: leading to severe confusion that prevents learning. In con\u0002trast, the impact during pretraining is far less severe in ZS\nL748@P7: MMEB. This is because the pretraining data consists of di\u0002verse query-target pairs for each image. While this still\nL749@P7: causes a minor performance drop due to potential semantic\nL750@P7: overlap between the pairs, the diversity prevents the learn\u0002ing collapse seen in the fine-tuning stage.\nL751@P7: Ablation study of the subsequent turn design for fine\u0002tuning on single-pair dataset. We compare other design\nL752@P7: choices for the subsequent turn used in the fine-tuning stage\nL753@P7: as shown in Tab. 8. First, we analyze the counterpart mask\u0002ing ratio, finding that 50% yields the best performance. A\nL754@P7: lower ratio (e.g. 25%) is suboptimal as the masked input is\nL755@P7: Table 8. Ablation study of the subsequent turn design for fine\u0002tuning on single-pair dataset. We analyze the counterpart mask\u0002ing ratio, the importance of reconstruction guidance (compared to\nL756@P7: no guidance or simple rephrasing), and find that the choice of im\u0002age captioning model has a minimal impact\nL757@P7: Setup MMEB\nL758@P7: Counterpart masking ratio 25% 69.3\nL759@P7: Counterpart masking ratio 50% 69.5\nL760@P7: Counterpart masking ratio 75% 68.9\nL761@P7: Rephrasing template 68.5\nL762@P7: Without reconstruction guidance 69.0\nL763@P7: Image captioning (BLIP Large [36]) 69.4\nL764@P7: Image captioning (Qwen2-VL-7B [60]) 69.5\nL765@P7: too similar to the original counterparts which provide only\nL766@P7: a minimal learning signal. Conversely, a higher ratio (e.g.\nL767@P7: 75%) also results in a larger performance drop because the\nL768@P7: recountruction task becomes excessively difficult.\nL769@P7: We also analyze the dialog template for the fine-tuning\nL770@P7: strategy. Interestingly, using just a simple rephrasing tem\u0002plate (User: Please rephrase your last response in embed\u0002ding space\\n Assistant: <|emb|>) outperforms the MuCo\nL771@P7: without compounded supervision (68.4 in Tab. 6). This\nL772@P7: demonstrates our compounded supervision can improve the\nL773@P7: performance even though a simple subsequent is employed.\nL774@P7: When training without reconstruction guidance, the perfor\u0002mance drops to 69.0 from 69.5, which confirms that the ex\u0002plicit reconstruction prompt is crucial for compelling the\nL775@P7: model to reason about the relationship between the pairs in\nL776@P7: Eq. 6. Finally, we observe that the choice of image caption\u0002ing model (BLIP-Large vs. Qwen2-VL-7B) has a minimal\nL777@P7: impact on performance.\nL778@P7: 6. Conclusion\nL779@P7: In this work, we propose Multi-turn Contrastive Learning\nL780@P7: (MuCo), a dialogue-inspired framework to overcome the\nL781@P7: limitations of conventional single-turn contrastive learning.\nL782@P7: By reframing representation learning as a multi-turn dia\u0002logue and modeling contextual dependencies, MuCo learns\nL783@P7: richer and more coherent embeddings than isolated pair\nL784@P7: alignment. Furthermore, MuCo tackles scalability bottle\u0002necks by processing multi-turn query sequences in a sin\u0002gle forward pass. This approach significantly reduces com\u0002putational overhead while increasing the effective batch\nL785@P7: size. Supported by our new 5M-scale M3T corpus, MuCo\nL786@P7: achieves new state-of-the-art performance on universal mul\u0002timodal embedding benchmarks. MuCo demonstrates that\nL787@P7: it is possible to simultaneously enhance model perfor\u0002mance and training scalability, effectively redefining the\nL788@P7: efficiency-capacity trade-off in multimodal alignment. We\nL789@P7: believe this work opens new avenues for more efficient and\nL790@P7-8: context-aware multimodal representation learning systems.Acknowledgment\nL791@P8: This work utilized Bruno from the NAVER AI Search Plat\u0002form to facilitate large-scale data processing tasks. We\nL792@P8: thank the platform for providing robust Model as a Service\nL793@P8: (MaaS) support.\nL794@P8: References\nL795@P8: [1] Sandhini Agarwal, Lama Ahmad, Jason Ai, Sam Altman,\nL796@P8: Andy Applebaum, Edwin Arbus, Rahul K Arora, Yu Bai,\nL797@P8: Bowen Baker, Haiming Bao, et al. gpt-oss-120b & gpt-oss\u000220b model card. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.10925, 2025. 2,\nL798@P8: 4, 15\nL799@P8: [2] Shuai Bai, Keqin Chen, Xuejing Liu, Jialin Wang, Wenbin\nL800@P8: Ge, Sibo Song, Kai Dang, Peng Wang, Shijie Wang, Jun\nL801@P8: Tang, et al. Qwen2. 5-vl technical report. arXiv preprint\nL802@P8: arXiv:2502.13923, 2025. 2, 3, 4, 15\nL803@P8: [3] Andrei Barbu, David Mayo, Julian Alverio, William Luo,\nL804@P8: Christopher Wang, Dan Gutfreund, Josh Tenenbaum, and\nL805@P8: Boris Katz. Objectnet: A large-scale bias-controlled dataset\nL806@P8: for pushing the limits of object recognition models. Ad\u0002vances in neural information processing systems, 32, 2019.\nL807@P8: 14\nL808@P8: [4] Yingshan Chang, Mridu Narang, Hisami Suzuki, Guihong\nL809@